# 108. Git-to-Server Runtime Alignment and Deployment Drift Guard

TitanGold must prevent a completed GitHub change from silently remaining absent
from the runtime environment that is expected to serve it.

The canonical principle is:

> Git completion, deployment completion and runtime verification are three
> separate facts.

Therefore:

`MERGED != DEPLOYED != VERIFIED`

A Git commit, green CI result, merged PR or documentation closeout must never be
treated as proof that the corresponding runtime is active on the server.

This section refines the deployment and provenance requirements in Section 29
and connects them to the Progress Snapshot in Section 107.

## 108.1 Runtime-Affecting vs Non-Runtime Changes

Every closeout must classify the change as one of:

- `DEPLOY_REQUIRED = YES`
- `DEPLOY_REQUIRED = NO`
- `DEPLOY_REQUIRED = UNKNOWN / BLOCKED`

`DEPLOY_REQUIRED = YES` applies when the completed outcome changes a runtime
artifact, runtime configuration or served product behavior, including where
applicable:

- Backend code
- Frontend code
- Worker or scheduler code
- Runtime configuration
- PM2 ecosystem or process configuration
- Nginx/runtime routing
- Database migration required by the runtime outcome
- Generated runtime artifact
- Provider/runtime adapter
- Any executable service or bundle expected to be active on the target server

`DEPLOY_REQUIRED = NO` is appropriate only when the completed change cannot alter
the served runtime, for example:

- Rule 02 governance-only correction
- Core Rule documentation-only update
- Documentation-only closeout
- Non-runtime evidence or planning artifact

A documentation commit must not trigger a meaningless redeployment solely to
make Git and runtime SHAs appear identical.

When `DEPLOY_REQUIRED = NO`, use:

```text
DEPLOY_STATUS = NOT_REQUIRED
RUNTIME_VERIFIED = NOT_APPLICABLE
SERVER_DRIFT = NONE
```

This means no runtime verification is required for that documentation/governance
change; it does not make any claim about unrelated runtime health.

## 108.2 Mandatory Runtime Alignment Fields

For every meaningful runtime-affecting closeout, record the following in the
current Evidence Ledger and, where useful, summarize them in Rule 02:

```text
MAIN_GIT_SHA = <canonical merged main baseline>
RUNTIME_IMPLEMENTATION_SHA = <implementation actually deployed or mapped>
DEPLOY_REQUIRED = YES | NO | UNKNOWN / BLOCKED
DEPLOY_STATUS = NOT_REQUIRED | PENDING | DEPLOYED | FAILED | ROLLED_BACK
RUNTIME_VERIFIED = YES | NO | NOT_APPLICABLE
SERVER_DRIFT = NONE | EXPECTED_TREE_EQUIVALENT | <short blocker/reason>
SERVED_ARTIFACT_PROVENANCE = <verified bundle/service/runtime reference>
```

Where the architecture uses multiple independently deployed artifacts, record
the relevant provenance separately, for example:

- Backend runtime commit
- Frontend served bundle
- Worker runtime commit
- Migration baseline
- PM2/runtime configuration provenance

Do not force unrelated runtime surfaces to share one fake SHA.

## 108.3 No Runtime Stage Closure Before Verification

When `DEPLOY_REQUIRED = YES`, the corresponding implementation or production
stage must not be marked complete merely because:

- Code was committed
- Code was pushed
- A PR was merged
- CI was green
- A build succeeded locally

Before the runtime-affecting stage is complete, verify as applicable:

1. The intended canonical implementation is deployed or proven tree-equivalent
   to the deployed artifact.
2. The correct server/runtime path is active.
3. Required process reload/restart/replacement occurred only where needed.
4. Health/readiness passes.
5. The served frontend/backend/worker artifact matches the intended baseline.
6. Relevant Browser/API/runtime QA passes.
7. No unexpected server drift remains.
8. Runtime provenance is recorded.

If any required item is unresolved:

```text
RUNTIME_VERIFIED = NO
SERVER_DRIFT = <reason>
```

and the stage remains open, blocked or `NEEDS MORE VERIFICATION`.

## 108.4 Post-Merge Runtime Reconciliation

After a runtime-affecting PR is merged, perform one bounded post-merge
reconciliation according to Section 29.

The preferred outcomes are:

### Exact-Main Deployment

The runtime is built/deployed from the exact canonical main merge commit.

Record:

```text
SERVER_DRIFT = NONE
```

### Proven Tree-Equivalent Deployment

A previously deployed implementation commit is proven to be runtime/tree
equivalent to the merged main product baseline.

This is allowed only when the proof is explicit and the served artifact is
verified.

Record:

```text
SERVER_DRIFT = EXPECTED_TREE_EQUIVALENT
```

with the implementation and main references in the Evidence Ledger.

Do not redeploy unchanged runtime merely to cosmetically equalize commit
markers.

### Real Drift

If GitHub main contains a runtime-affecting change that is not present on the
expected server/runtime:

```text
SERVER_DRIFT = RUNTIME_ALIGNMENT_PENDING
RUNTIME_VERIFIED = NO
```

The affected milestone must not be called complete.

## 108.5 Deployment Failure and Rollback Truthfulness

If deployment fails:

- Do not claim the feature is live.
- Record the failed deployment state.
- Preserve the last verified runtime baseline.
- Follow the approved rollback or recovery path.
- Reverify runtime state after rollback.

If rollback restores the previous runtime successfully, distinguish:

- Git product state: newer merged implementation exists
- Runtime state: older verified implementation remains active

This is a real Git-to-server drift and must remain visible until explicitly
resolved.

Do not hide this state by changing documentation markers.

## 108.6 Runtime Drift as a Progress Blocker

Section 107 Progress Snapshot must treat unresolved required runtime alignment as
a real blocker.

When the active stage requires server deployment and runtime verification is not
complete:

```text
PROGRESS_BLOCKER = RUNTIME_ALIGNMENT_PENDING
```

or another precise runtime blocker.

Do not advance the counted stage merely because the Git portion is finished.

When deployment is not required, do not create a fake deployment blocker.

## 108.7 Cursor Closeout Requirement

For every meaningful Cursor closeout, include one compact deployment line:

```text
RUNTIME: Deploy <YES/NO/UNKNOWN> | Status <state> | Verified <YES/NO/NOT_APPLICABLE> | Drift <NONE/reason>
```

For runtime-affecting work, the closeout must also identify the canonical Git
baseline and actual deployed/runtime provenance.

The Integrator must verify that the runtime line agrees with:

- Git state
- Runtime evidence
- Rule 02
- Evidence Ledger
- Progress Snapshot

If these disagree, perform reconciliation before declaring the stage complete.

## 108.8 No Automatic Production Mutation from Documentation

Adding this Rule does not authorize deployment, restart, configuration mutation,
database migration or production access.

Deployment remains governed by:

- Dynamic Active-Work authorization
- Risk tier
- Existing production-safety rules
- Required Human QA
- Operation-specific approval where applicable

This section is a verification and closure requirement, not standing production
authorization.

## 108.9 Final Git-to-Server Alignment Principle

The goal is not to make Git and server markers look identical.

The goal is:

> Every runtime-affecting change that TitanGold calls complete must be proven to
> exist in the intended runtime, and every intentional or accidental difference
> between Git and the server must remain explicit until reconciled.

For owner visibility:

> A feature is not finished merely because it exists on GitHub. It is finished
> when the intended runtime is deployed, verified and truthfully mapped back to
> the canonical Git state.

